Our approach bears a structural analogy to guidance distillation in diffusion models. In classifier-guided diffusion, an external classifier provides a gradient signal $\nabla_{x_t} \log p(y | x_t)$ that steers the sampling process toward a desired class $y$. Guidance distillation \citep{meng2023distillation} trains a single model to reproduce the effect of this guidance without requiring the classifier at inference. Similarly, \ours{} uses the SDF auxiliary head as a ``geometric safety classifier'' that provides a training-time gradient signal directing the velocity field away from collision regions. After training, the model has internalized this signal, and the auxiliary head (analogous to the classifier) is no longer needed.

Unlike classical guidance distillation, which replicates a guided sampling trajectory during training, our method shapes the velocity field directly through auxiliary gradients. The SDF head provides a training-time geometric signal that modifies the learned flow rather than distilling a pre-computed guided process. A further difference is that our method operates in the flow-matching framework rather than score-based diffusion, and the guidance signal comes from geometric structure (signed distances) rather than semantic class labels.

